<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our New Classmates – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our New Classmates</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> What was Your Previous School Like?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Write a personal diary entry about a recent event. Interactive: Reply to a message written on paper, in a text app, or in an email.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a personal diary entry about a recent event at school or in the community (e.g., "Today, I joined a new class. I felt nervous at first, but then I met Juan Manuel, and we became friends."). Can reply to a simple message from a classmate about school activities (e.g., "Hi Tammy! Yes, I will bring my notebook for the science project tomorrow. See you in class!"). Can describe a recent move from one school to another in a short email to a friend (e.g., "Hello, Bill. This is Juan Carlos. Recently, I moved to a new school in Chiriqui. It is bigger than my old school in Puerto Armueles. It is more modern, quieter, and cleaner. I will make new friends here!").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing pictures of different schools (e.g., a small rural school, a large city school, a modern school, an old school). Students observe the pictures and think about their previous schools. The teacher asks students to quickly write down two adjectives that describe each school picture.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to describe a school using comparatives and superlatives. For example, "My old school was small. This school is bigger than my old school. It is the most modern school I have seen." The teacher writes these sentences on the board, pointing out the comparative and superlative structures and key vocabulary like 'bigger,' 'more modern,' 'old,' 'new,' 'small,' 'clean,' 'noisy.' The teacher also emphasizes the endings for plural nouns and third person singular verbs, like 'schools' /z/ and 'watches' /ɪz/ if relevant to the modeled sentences.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'bigger' for comparing two schools or many schools?" (Expected answer: "Two schools.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What does 'most modern' mean?" (Expected answer: "It is the number one modern school, no other school is more modern.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short email from a new classmate to a friend describing their move to a new school. The email will contain examples of Present Simple and Past Simple for descriptions and habits, and comparatives/superlatives. (e.g., "Hello, Bill. This is Juan Carlos. Recently, I moved to a new school in Chiriqui. It is bigger than my old school in Puerto Armueles. It is more modern, quieter, and cleaner. I will make new friends here! My old school was small, but it had a big playground. Here, the cafeteria is much larger.")</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Present personal and classmate information in group discussions using appropriate language. Responding to questions about classmates and schools. Comparing schools, classmates, or communities using comparatives and superlatives.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Writing focus):</b> Students read the email individually and complete the following tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What is the main idea of Juan Carlos's email?" (Students write a short answer.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match descriptions from the email to either "New School" or "Old School" (e.g., "bigger cafeteria" -> New School, "small playground" -> Old School).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing descriptive sentences and short paragraphs using Present and Past Simple for descriptions, comparatives and superlatives to compare schools and school activities, and future tense to express expectations. Vocabulary includes: <i>classmate, school, community, subject, classroom, schedule, recess, cafeteria, playground, friend, teacher, project, uniform, library, activity, new, friendly, nervous, excited, big, small, clean, noisy, quiet, interesting, boring, modern, previous, used to, come from, move from.</i></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled practice (Sentence Completion):</b> The teacher provides sentences with blanks, requiring students to complete them using the correct form of adjectives for comparatives/superlatives or appropriate past/present simple verbs based on context. Example: "My new school is ______ (big) than my old one." "I ______ (study) English every day." "She ______ (move from) Santiago last month." Students write their answers individually.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled practice (Guided Paragraph Writing):</b> Students are given sentence starters and prompts to write short paragraphs comparing aspects of their previous school with their current school. For example: "My old school was... but my new school is... (use comparatives)." "In my previous school, we used to... Now, we... (use Past/Present Simple)." "I will... at my new school." The teacher guides them to use the target grammar and vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Peer Review & Correction):</b> Students work in pairs. Each student shares their guided paragraphs with their partner. The partner reads and identifies two sentences that effectively use comparatives/superlatives and two sentences that correctly use Past/Present Simple or Future tense. They also suggest one minor correction (e.g., spelling, verb form) focusing on writing accuracy.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our New Classmates".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format: Writing an Email to a Friend or a Diary Entry</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students choose one of the following tasks:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option A (Email):</b> Write a short email (50-70 words) to a friend about a hypothetical recent move to a new school or a new classmate joining the class. The email must compare the new school/classmate with the old one, describe feelings (e.g., nervous, excited), and mention future plans or expectations. Students must use at least two comparatives/superlatives, one sentence in Past Simple, one in Present Simple, and one in Future Tense. They should incorporate at least 5 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>new, friendly, big, modern, learn, join, classmate, project</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Option B (Diary Entry):</b> Write a personal diary entry (50-70 words) about a recent event at school related to new classmates or a new school experience. The entry should describe the event, express personal feelings (e.g., nervous, excited), and include thoughts about the past (e.g., "I used to...") and future plans. Students must use at least two comparatives/superlatives, one sentence in Past Simple, one in Present Simple, and one in Future Tense. They should incorporate at least 5 target vocabulary words.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students focus on communicating their ideas clearly and appropriately for the chosen format, emphasizing fluency in written expression and the integration of target language.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and peer assessment.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students exchange their written email/diary entry with another classmate (not their previous partner). They use a simple checklist to provide feedback on specific criteria. The teacher also collects the written products for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Peer Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did the writing clearly describe a new school/classmate? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did it use at least two comparatives/superlatives correctly? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did it include sentences in Past, Present, and Future Tense? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did it include at least 5 target vocabulary words? (Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Is the message clear and easy to understand? (Yes/No)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts on a small piece of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I wrote well today was..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I want to improve in my writing next time is..."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do I feel now writing about my previous school or a new classmate using comparatives and different tenses?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing today's email/diary entry was most challenging for me?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The writing activities worked well for students to practice grammar and vocabulary. The peer feedback helped students identify areas for improvement. Some students struggled with the correct usage of future tense, which needs more reinforcement. Additionally, ensuring all students correctly use the /s/, /z/, and /ɪz/ endings for plurals and third-person singular verbs in their mental composition before writing should be a consistent reminder. Next time, I will provide more explicit sentence frames for the future tense and review the pronunciation of these endings again as a quick warm-up for writing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> The next lesson (Lesson 5: Mediation & Integration) will build on describing previous and new school experiences by asking students to mediate information about school life to new classmates in a collaborative project, consolidating their understanding of the theme and linguistic structures.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>